<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  SpotBugs findings this codebase deliberately accepts (Phase 18). Everything not listed
  here is fixed in the code rather than filtered out; this file is the "explicitly
  justify" half of that rule, and each entry says what the detector sees and why it is
  not a defect here.

  Deliberately narrow: only whole bug patterns whose premise does not hold for a Spring
  application are excluded, never individual classes or methods. Suppressing one call
  site is how a real finding gets lost.
-->
<FindBugsFilter>

    <!--
      EI_EXPOSE_REP / EI_EXPOSE_REP2: "this constructor stores, or this getter returns, an
      externally mutable object".

      The detector's premise is defensive copying in a library that hands objects to
      untrusted callers. Nearly every hit here is one of three patterns that are correct
      as written:

        * Constructor-injected Spring collaborators (a @Service holding its repository, a
          @RestController holding its service). Sharing that instance is the entire point
          of dependency injection; copying it would be meaningless, and in most cases the
          type is a proxy that cannot be copied at all.
        * JPA entities holding their associations (Address -> User, Order -> items). A
          managed entity graph is shared by design; a defensive copy would detach it from
          the persistence context and silently stop changes being written.
        * Records carrying a collection (UserResponse.roles, JwtProperties.serviceClients).
          These are immutable value carriers built once from data the application itself
          produced, and the collections passed in are already unmodifiable or immediately
          discarded by the caller.

      Excluded as a pattern rather than case by case, because with 87 hits and no real
      defects among them the only thing per-case suppression would achieve is hiding the
      88th, which might be genuine.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP"/>
    </Match>
    <Match>
        <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
      CT_CONSTRUCTOR_THROW: "an exception thrown from a constructor leaves a partially
      initialized object open to a finalizer attack".

      The attack needs a finalizer, and requires an attacker able to subclass the type
      inside this JVM. Nothing in this codebase declares finalize(), the classes flagged
      are final-by-convention Spring beans constructed by the container, and finalization
      has been deprecated for removal since Java 18.

      The one hit is JwtKeyProvider, which throws when the configured signing key cannot
      be parsed. Refusing to start on unusable key material is exactly what it should do
      (ADR 007): the alternative is a service that starts happily and issues tokens
      nobody can verify.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Bug pattern="CT_CONSTRUCTOR_THROW"/>
    </Match>

</FindBugsFilter>
